B6 \u2192 Wait, this seems incorrect because B4 and B6 were used in [B4,B5,B6], so unless you have duplicates, you shouldn't hold them again.\n\nBut according to input: \"Your hand tiles are: [W2: 1; W4: 1; W6: 1; B4: 1; B6: 1]\" \u2014 this implies after forming [B4,B5,B6] as a meld, you still hold B4 and B6? That can't happen unless there are duplicate tiles.\n\nHowever, each tile has 4 copies. It's possible: someone else discarded B4 or B6, and you picked it up later. But let's assume the state is correct \u2014 you have extra B4 and B6 in hand.\n\nBut more importantly, your hand shows only singletons with no clear path to a pair.\n\nBest strategy: discard safely while keeping flexibility.\n\nAmong valid actions:\n- **Play B4**: B4 appears 3 times unshown, so not dead. Risky.\n- **Play B6**: Only 1 left unshown, and B7 is gone \u2014 likely B6 is dangerous (someone may be completing 456/678). Avoid.\n- **Play W2**: 2 still available, not obviously dangerous. But not ideal.\n- **Play W4**: 3 available \u2014 possible, but opponents have shown interest in middle W tiles.\n- **Play W6**: **0 remaining in wall**, meaning all four copies are accounted for: \n    - You have one,\n    - You used one in your [W5,W6,W7],\n    - Opponent has [W7,W7,W7], and previous player had [W3,W4,W5] \u2014 where did the fourth W6 go?\n    - Unshown count says W6: 2 \u2014 wait, contradiction?\n\nWait! There is inconsistency.\n\nGiven: \"Unshown tiles are: ... W6: 2 ...\" \u2014 so **two W6 remain unshown**.\n\nBut your meld is [W5, W6, W7] \u2014 you used a W6.\nYour hand has W6:1 \u2014 second W6.\nThat\u2019s two W6 accounted for.\n\nTwo more must exist originally: total 4.\nSo 4 - 2 = 2 unshown \u2014 matches.\n\nBut then why is W7 at 0 unshown?\nYour meld uses W7, next player has [W7,W7,W7] \u2014 that\u2019s four W7s already. So yes, W7 is exhausted.\n\nSimilarly, W3: 0 unshown \u2014 previous player used in [W3,W4,W5], and you don\u2019t see others, so likely all used.\n\nBack to decision:\n\nW6: 2 unshown \u2192 possible danger.\n\nBut look at history:\n- You Chowed W6 earlier \u2192 you claimed a discard to complete [W5,W6,W7]\n- Since then, no one has discarded W6\n- But W6 is not dead\n\nNow consider **safe discards**.\n\nNote: J1 is completely gone (0 unshown) \u2014 all 4 used.\nF1: 1 left\nF3: 2 left\nF4: 1 left\nJ2: 1 left\nJ3: 3 left \u2014 very live\n\nBut none of these help.\n\nYou need to draw and improve.\n\nCurrently, your hand is fragmented. No pair in sight.\n\nPossible improvement:\n- Draw W3? Gone.\n- Draw W5? Possible (2 left), but W5-W6-W7 already used.\n- Maybe aim for **Pure Straight**? Need 123, 456, 789 in same suit.\nYou have 567 (via [W5,W6,W7]) \u2014 good start.\nIf you could get [W1,W2,W3] and [W4,W5,W6] or similar \u2014 but W3 is gone.\n\nImpossible.\n\nAlternatively, **Mixed Straight**: 123, 456, 789 in different suits.\nYou have:\n- [W5,W6,W7] \u2192 counts as 567\nNeed 123 and 789.\n\nDo you have any 123? Not yet.\nBut you hold W2, W4 \u2014 could draw W1/W3 for 123? W3 is gone.\n\nNo.\n\nAlternatively, **All Pungs**? Not close.\n\nBest idea: minimize risk.\n\nLook at **B6**: only 1 unshown \u2014 very dangerous. Likely someone has a use for it (e.g., [B4,B5,B6] or [B6,B7,B8]). The across player has [B5,B6,B7] \u2014 they completed it already. So B6 might be safer.\n\nBut they discarded B8 \u2014 perhaps not holding B6 anymore.\n\nStill, better to avoid.\n\nNow, **W4**: unshown 3 \u2192 relatively safe.\n\nBut previous player has [W3,W4,W5] \u2014 they completed it. They\u2019ve discarded W6, W4, etc. Probably don\u2019t need W4.\n\nBut you have W4 in hand \u2014 could be useful if drawing W3 or W5 to form another chow.\n\nBut W3 is gone. W5: 2 left.\n\nSo W4 + draw W5 \u2192 could make [W4,W5,W6] if you have W6 \u2014 but you do!\n\nYou have W4 and W6 \u2014 if you draw W5, you can form [W4,W5,W6] as a Chow!\n\nThat\u2019s promising.\n\nSimilarly, if you keep W2, and draw W1/W3 \u2014 but W3 is gone, W1: 2 left \u2014 possible [W1,W2,W3]? But W3 gone \u2014 impossible.\n\nSo only viable improvement: draw W5 \u2192 complete [W4,W5,W6] chow.\n\nThen your hand becomes:\n- Melds: [W5,W6,W7], [B4,B5,B6], [F2,F2,F2], [W4,W5,W6] \u2192 wait, conflict over W5 and W6?\n\nNo \u2014 you cannot reuse tiles.\n\nProblem: your existing meld [W5,W6,W7] already uses one W5, one W6, one W7.\n\nYou cannot use W5 and W6 again.\n\nUnless you have duplicates.\n\nCheck counts:\n- W5: 2 unshown \u2192 total 4, so 2 already out.\n- Your meld used one W5, one W6, one W7.\n- Previous player\u2019s [W3,W4,W5] used one W5.\n- So two W5 used \u2014 two left unshown \u2192 okay.\n- But if you want to form [W4,W5,W6], you need another W5 and W6.\n\nYou have W4 and W6 in hand \u2014 that\u2019s one each.\n\nSo if you draw W5, you can indeed form [W4,W5,W6] as a new chow \u2014 using the second copy.\n\nYes! Because there are 4 copies.\n\nSo:\n- Copy 1: used in your [W5,W6,W7]\n- Copy 2: used in opponent\u2019s [W3,W4,W5]\n- Copies 3 and 4: still available\n\nYou have W4 and W6 in hand \u2014 likely one of the remaining copies.\n\nSo if you draw W5 (2 available), you can Chi on it (if from left) or claim to form chow.\n\nThus, keeping W4 and W6 gives chance to form second chow in W suit.\n\nBetter than holding B4/B6, which are bamboo.\n\nAlso, **Half Flush** (12 points): hand consists of one suit and honor tiles.\nYou have:\n- Melds: [W5,W6,W7], possibly [W4,W5,W6] \u2192 both in W\n- [F2,F2,F2] \u2192 honors\n- [B4,B5,B6] \u2192 problem \u2014 this is Bamboo suit\n\nAh \u2014 **[B4,B5,B6] is in a different suit**, so it breaks Half Flush.\n\nTo qualify for Half Flush, all suited tiles must be in one suit.\n\nBut you have both W and B melds \u2192 cannot get Half Flush.\n\nSimilarly, **Full Flush** requires all suited tiles same suit \u2014 impossible due to mixed W and B.\n\nSo best hope is **All Pungs** (6 points) or **Mixed Straight** (8 points).\n\nBut you have no 123 or 789 chows.\n\nAlternatively, **Mixed Shifted Chows**: e.g., W345, B456, T567 \u2014 you have B456 and W567 \u2014 close.\n\nIf you had T567, that would work.\n\nBut you don\u2019t.\n\nAnd you have F2 pung.\n\nNot helpful.\n\nAnother idea: **discard a Bamboo tile** to reduce clutter.\n\nBetween **B4** and **B6**, which is safer?\n\nB4: 3 unshown \u2014 fairly live\nB6: 1 unshown \u2014 more likely to be needed\n\nBut B5: 2 unshown \u2014 still around\n\nAcross player has [B5,B6,B7] \u2014 they completed it, and discarded B8, B2, etc. \u2014 probably safe.\n\nBut better to play **B4** or **B6**?\n\nWait \u2014 you have **B4** and **B6** in hand, but your meld is [B4,B5,B6] \u2014 so you used one of each.\n\nNow you have extra B4 and B6 \u2014 possibly building toward [B4,B4,B4] or [B6,B6,B6]?\n\nBut no signs of kong.\n\nAnd J1, J2, F1 all scarce.\n\nBut **Dragon Pung**: you have F2 (Wind), not Dragon.\n\nJ1 is Red Dragon \u2014 0 left \u2014 all used up. Someone likely has a kong or two pungs.\n\nBut not relevant.\n\nBest move: **discard W2**\n\nWhy?\n- W2: 2 unshown \u2014 average danger\n- Cannot be used in any chow you can complete (need W1/W3 for 123 or 234 \u2014 W3 gone)\n- So W2 is dead-ended\n- Whereas W4 and W6 can form [W4,W5,W6] if you draw W5\n\nSo **keep W4 and W6** for potential chow\n\nKeep B4/B6? Less valuable \u2014 different suit\n\nBut you must choose among:\n[Play B4; Play B6; Play W2; Play W4; Play W6]\n\nSo best is to **play W2** \u2014 least useful for improvement.\n\nAlternatively, **play B6** \u2014 since B6 is rarer (only 1 unshown), but you have no way to know who holds it.\n\nBut **W2** is safer to discard because:\n- No one has shown interest in low W tiles recently\n- High W tiles (5-7) are active\n- Low W tiles less contested\n\nWhereas B4 and B6 \u2014 middle bamboo \u2014 always useful\n\nSo discard **W2**\n\nFinal choice: **Play W2**\n\nEvaluation: Among the valid actions, discarding W2 is the safest and most logical, as W2 cannot contribute to any viable chow (W3 is exhausted), while W4 and W6 are key to potentially forming a [W4,W5,W6] chow if W5 is drawn. B4 and B6 are part of the bamboo suit, which is already represented in an exposed meld, and retaining them offers little advantage compared to pursuing additional character suit combinations. W2 has minimal utility and poses low risk when discarded.\n\nReason: W2 is unlikely to complete an opponent's hand and cannot be used by me to form a new meld due to the absence of W3. Keeping W4 and W6 preserves the chance to complete a [W4,W5,W6] chow upon drawing W5, improving hand structure toward patterns like Mixed Shifted Chows or simply reducing loose tiles.\n\nAnswer: Play W2"}
