r, you get **All Chows** (2 points)\n- But currently no pair\n- Could form pair from W6, T3, B4, B5, B6\n\nBest candidates for pair: W6 (you have one), or draw another W6, B4, etc.\n\nBut W6: only one left unshown (unshown=0? Wait no: unshown W6:0 \u2192 all four are accounted for)\n\nW6: total 4\n- Used in your meld [W5,W6,W7]: 1\n- You played W6: 1\n- Previous player played W6: 1\n- ? Missing one\n\nEarlier calculation issue.\n\nUnshown W6:0 \u2192 all 4 are visible.\n\nBut:\n- Your meld shows one W6\n- You played one W6\n- Previous player played one W6\n\u2192 3\n\nOne missing.\n\nUnless the tile you claimed in Chi was from a player who had two, but still.\n\nPossibly, the next player or across player discarded or used it.\n\nAcross player: played W1, W2, W3, W4, W4 \u2014 no W6\n\nNext player: played W7, but not W6\n\nBut next player has [W7,W7,W7] meld \u2014 three W7\n\nW7: unshown=0 \u2192 all gone\n\nUsed:\n- Your meld [W5,W6,W7]: one W7\n- You played W7? No\n- Previous player played W7\n- Across player played W7? No\n- Next player melded three W7 \u2192 three\n- Your meld: one \u2192 total 4\n- Yes: next player has three, your meld has one \u2192 total 4 \u2192 unshown=0 \u2192 correct\n\nBack to W6: unshown=0\n\nSources:\n- Your meld: 1\n- You played: 1 (history: \"Play W6\")\n- Previous player played: 1 (\"Play W6\")\n\u2192 3\n\nMissing one.\n\nPerhaps you played W6 twice?\n\nHistory: only one \"Play W6\"\n\nUnless typo.\n\nAssume the data is correct.\n\nPossibility: when you Chi W6, you expose it, and that's one.\n\nThen you later drew another W6 and discarded it \u2014 second.\n\nPrevious player discarded one \u2014 third.\n\nAnd... did the across player or next player discard?\n\nNot in history.\n\nBut unshown=0 \u2192 must be four.\n\nSo likely, one more W6 is in discard pile not listed.\n\nAnyway, unshown=0 \u2192 no W6 left \u2192 you cannot draw another.\n\nTherefore, the W6 in your hand now is the last one, and playing it is safe \u2014 no one can use it for a chow, because:\n- To complete [W4,W5,W6]: needs W6, but W5 is available (2 unshown), W4 only 1 \u2014 possible, but unlikely\n- To complete [W6,W7,W8]: W7 is all gone (0), so impossible\n- To complete [W5,W6,W7]: W7 gone, impossible\n\nSo W6 can only be used for [W4,W5,W6], which requires W4 and W5.\n\nW4: only 1 left, W5: 2 left.\n\nBut someone might be waiting on W6 for that.\n\nBut with W7 completely gone, and W8 heavily used (across player has [W8,W8,W8]), it's unlikely.\n\nSimilarly, B4: unshown=3 \u2192 many left\n\nB4 can be used in [B2,B3,B4], [B3,B4,B5], [B4,B5,B6]\n\nBut [B4,B5,B6] is already held by you and across player has [B5,B6,B7], so B5 and B6 are popular.\n\nDiscarding B4 might be dangerous if someone is building lower chows.\n\nB6: unshown=1 \u2192 only one left (you hold it)\n\nB6 can be used in [B4,B5,B6], [B5,B6,B7], [B6,B7,B8]\n\nBut B7: unshown=0 \u2192 all gone (you played two, previous played, across melded three? Wait)\n\nB7: total 4\n- You played B7 twice (history)\n- Previous player played B7\n- Across player melded [B5,B6,B7] \u2192 one B7\n\u2192 4 \u2192 yes, all gone\n\nSo B7=0 \u2192 cannot be used in any chow requiring B7\n\nThus, B6 can only be used in [B4,B5,B6] or [B5,B6,B7]\n\nBut [B5,B6,B7] requires B7 \u2192 impossible\n\nSo only [B4,B5,B6]\n\nB5: unshown=2 \u2192 possible\n\nBut you already have [B4,B5,B6] meld, and across has [B5,B6,B7], so B5 is active.\n\nBut someone might be waiting on B6 for [B4,B5,B6]\n\nBut since B6 is the only one left, and you hold it, discarding it might be safe if no one is waiting specifically on B6.\n\nBut riskier than W6.\n\nT3: unshown=2\n\nCan be used in [T1,T2,T3], [T2,T3,T4], [T3,T4,T5]\n\nT1: unshown=2, T2:1, T4:3 \u2014 so possible\n\nBut T2: only one left, so [T1,T2,T3] requires T2 \u2014 scarce\n\n[T2,T3,T4] also needs T2\n\n[T3,T4,T5] possible\n\nBut no one has shown strong interest in T-suit except you played T1,T2,T8,T9\n\nSo T3 discard is medium risk.\n\nNow, best discard:\n\n- **Play W6**: W6 is safest because W7 is gone, so no higher chow possible. Only [W4,W5,W6] remains, but W4 is low (1 left), and no one has shown interest recently. Also, you already have a W6 in meld, so value is low.\n\n- **Play B6**: similar, but B5 is still available, and B4 is common, so [B4,B5,B6] is possible. Riskier.\n\n- **Play B4**: opens [B4,B5,B6], and B4 is common, so high risk.\n\n- **Play B5**: central tile, very useful for chows, high risk.\n\n- **Play T3**: mid-low, but T2 is scarce, so [T1,T2,T3] unlikely. Safer than B-tiles.\n\nThus, safest is **W6** or **T3**.\n\nBut you have W6 in hand, and no use for it (already have the chow), and W7 gone, so W6 is dead tile.\n\nSimilarly, T3 might help someone.\n\nAlso, you might want to keep B4,B5,B6 together to potentially form a new chow if you draw matching tiles, but you already have the meld, so no.\n\nYou need a pair.\n\nIf you keep B4,B5,B6, and draw another B5, you can have a pair.\n\nBut better to keep one suit group.\n\nBut currently, discarding W6 does not break any potential.\n\nMoreover, W6 is less useful for future draws.\n\nSo best to discard W6.\n\nIt also helps avoid giving away useful tiles.\n\nFinal decision: **Play W6**\n\nEvaluation: Among the options, W6 is the safest discard because the completion of any chow containing W6 (especially [W6,W7,W8]) is impossible due to W7 being completely depleted (0 unshown). Other tiles like B4, B5, B6 are still viable for common chows like [B4,B5,B6], making them riskier to discard. T3 is moderately safe but could contribute to lower bamboo sequences.\n\nReason: W6 is a dead tile with no further utility for opponents due to the absence of W7, reducing the risk of feeding a winning hand. Discarding it maintains safety while preserving potentially useful tiles in the B-dot and T-bamboo suits for future pairing opportunities.\n\nAnswer: Play W6"}
