 tiles temporarily, now must discard one.\n\nYes! That fits: Before draw, I had 13 - 9 = 4 tiles in hand. Now I drew one \u2192 5 tiles \u2192 must discard one.\n\nSo hand: [W8, T5, B4, B5, B6] \u2014 five tiles including the newly drawn one.\n\nBut again: if [B4,B5,B6] is already a meld, I cannot have B4, B5, B6 in hand unless I have duplicates.\n\nBut B4 unshown:3 \u2192 4 total - 3 unseen = 1 shown/used. If my meld used one B4, and I have another in hand, that\u2019s two total \u2192 possible only if I have two copies. But hand says B4:1 \u2192 one copy.\n\nSame for B5: unshown:2 \u2192 2 unseen \u2192 2 used? My meld uses one, hand has one \u2192 total two \u2192 possible.\n\nB6: unshown:1 \u2192 3 used? My meld one, hand one \u2192 two \u2192 unshown:1 \u2192 total three \u2192 implies only three used \u2192 possible.\n\nBut maximum per tile is 4. So possible if:\n- B4: 1 in meld, 1 in hand \u2192 2 total \u2192 2 unseen \u2192 but unshown says 3 \u2192 contradiction.\n\nUnshown B4:3 \u2192 means 3 of B4 remain unseen (not discarded, not in visible melds). Total copies: 4. So 4 - 3 = 1 already revealed (discarded or in exposed melds).\n\nMy meld [B4,B5,B6] is exposed (from Chi) \u2192 so B4 is revealed \u2192 counts toward the one revealed \u2192 unseen:3 \u2192 ok.\n\nNow, if I have B4 in hand \u2192 that\u2019s a second B4 \u2192 so total two B4s accounted: one in meld, one in hand \u2192 then unseen should be 2 \u2192 but stated unshown:3 \u2192 contradiction.\n\nTherefore, **I cannot have B4 in hand if I already used one in an exposed meld** \u2014 unless there are four copies and others are hidden.\n\nWait \u2014 unless the meld [B4,B5,B6] is not using my own tiles exclusively? No, it's *my* meld.\n\nOnly resolution: **The hand list includes tiles that are not part of melds**. So if I have [B4,B5,B6] as a meld, I should not have B4, B5, B6 individually in hand.\n\nBut the system presents hand as [W8, T5, B4, B5, B6] \u2014 likely a display bug or misrepresentation.\n\nAlternative interpretation: perhaps the meld [B4,B5,B6] is not mine? But it says \"Your melds\".\n\nBest assumption: despite the apparent conflict, accept the data as given \u2014 possibly the hand includes tiles that could be used in multiple ways, but logically, **having a meld [B4,B5,B6] means those three tiles are no longer in hand**.\n\nTherefore, the hand tiles listed must exclude those in melds.\n\nSo likely, the hand is actually: [W8, T5] plus three other tiles, but displayed incorrectly.\n\nBut we must proceed.\n\nGiven the valid actions are: [Play B4; Play B6; Play B5; Play T5; Play W8]\n\nThis implies I can legally discard B4, B5, B6, T5, or W8 \u2014 so I must possess them.\n\nPossibility: I have multiple copies? Unshown B4:3 \u2192 so up to 3 elsewhere.\n\nBut I have one in meld \u2192 one in hand \u2192 total two \u2192 unseen:2 \u2192 but stated unshown:3 \u2192 impossible.\n\nUnless \u201cunshown\u201d means not currently visible on table, excluding hand.\n\nNo \u2014 typically \u201cunshown\u201d means not in open melds or discard piles \u2014 so includes hand and wall.\n\nSo if I have B4 in hand, and one in my meld, total two \u2192 unshown should be 2 \u2192 but says 3 \u2192 contradiction.\n\nOnly way: the meld [B4,B5,B6] is not mine? But it says \"Your melds\".\n\nPerhaps \"Your melds\" includes melds you participated in? No, standardly it's your own.\n\nAfter careful thought: accept the input as is \u2014 likely a system where hand and melds are listed separately regardless of overlap, but logically, **tiles in melds are not in hand**.\n\nSo probably, the hand is: [W8, T5] and three others, but the B4/B5/B6 in hand are mistakes.\n\nBut valid actions include Play B4 etc. \u2014 so I must have them.\n\nFinal decision: assume the data is correct and I have access to these tiles, possibly due to kong replacement or draw.\n\nBut safest move:\n\nFocus on **T5**: \n- Not a terminal/honor.\n- Part of \"All Fives\" pattern? But I have only T5, W5 in meld, B5 in meld \u2014 so W5, B5, T5 present \u2014 but need all melds to be fives \u2014 not the case.\n- No strong incentive to keep T5.\n- Less likely to complete others\u2019 hands compared to B4/B5/B6 which can form chows like 3-4-5, 4-5-6, 5-6-7.\n- B6 is dangerous because B7 is frequently discarded (player across has B7 in meld), so someone may have 5-6 ready.\n- Similarly, B4 could complete 2-3-4 or 3-4-5.\n- T5 is mid-game, less impactful.\n\nMoreover, **Seat Wind is South (F2)**, and I have Peng F2 \u2014 so I score **Seat Wind** (2 points). Also, **Prevailing Wind East (F1)** \u2014 I don\u2019t have F1, so no Prevalent Wind.\n\nOther patterns: I have three pungs \u2014 F2, W5-6-7 (sequence), B4-5-6 (sequence) \u2014 not valuable for All Pungs (6 pts) because two are chows.\n\nWait \u2014 [W5,W6,W7] is a chow, [B4,B5,B6] is a chow, [F2,F2,F2] is pung \u2014 so two chows, one pung \u2014 not close to All Pungs.\n\nHand type: mostly chows \u2014 aiming for All Chows? Possible, but I have a pung.\n\nWith T5, I could aim for T4-T5-T6, but T4 is available (unshown:3), T6:2 \u2014 possible, but slow.\n\nBetter to cut loose ends.\n\n**W8 is extremely dangerous** \u2014 across player has Peng W8, so three copies down, I discard the last \u2014 instant win for anyone needing it.\n\nSimilarly, F1, F2, F3, F4 are all partially gone \u2014 F1: prev player Peng, F2: I Peng, F3:1 unshown, F4:1 unshown.\n\nBack to discard choice.\n\nBest option: **Play T5** \u2014 least dangerous, least useful to others, and doesn't support strong patterns.\n\nAlternatively, **Play B5** \u2014 but B5 is in center, can complete 3-4-5, 4-5-6, 5-6-7 \u2014 high utility for opponents.\n\nSame with B4 and B6.\n\nT5 is safer.\n\nAlso, T5 unshown:2 \u2192 two still out \u2014 so not rare.\n\nBut no evidence of T-suit chows being built.\n\nWhereas B-suit: across has B5,B6,B7; I have B4,B5,B6; so B5 is hot.\n\nThus, **discard T5**.\n\nEvaluation: Among the valid actions, discarding T5